  "base64-encoder/content/explainer.md": "# How Base64 Encoding and Decoding Works\n\nBase64 is a binary-to-text encoding scheme defined in RFC 4648 that represents arbitrary binary sequences using a compact set of 64 printable ASCII characters (`A-Z`, `a-z`, `0-9`, `+`, and `/`).\n\n## The Encoding Mechanics\nEvery 3 bytes of binary data (24 bits) are split into four 6-bit chunks. Each 6-bit integer (ranging from 0 to 63) maps to an index in the Base64 alphabet:\n- Bits `0–25` represent `A–Z`\n- Bits `26–51` represent `a–z`\n- Bits `52–61` represent `0–9`\n- Bit `62` represents `+` (or `-` in URL-safe mode)\n- Bit `63` represents `/` (or `_` in URL-safe mode)\n\nWhen the total input byte count is not divisible by 3, one or two `=` characters are appended as padding to complete the 4-character alignment boundary.\n\n## UTF-8 Safe Architecture\nStandard legacy web APIs like `window.btoa` and `window.atob` operate strictly on 8-bit Latin-1 strings and throw an uncaught `InvalidCharacterError` when encountering multi-byte Unicode codepoints (such as accented vowels, Asian scripts, or emojis). This tool utilizes native `TextEncoder` and `TextDecoder` pipelines combined with chunked binary buffers, guaranteeing 100% loss-free representation across all international character sets.\n\n## URL-Safe and MIME Formats\nWeb query parameters and filenames frequently disallow `+` and `/` characters. Enabling URL-safe formatting replaces these symbols with hyphen-minus (`-`) and underscore (`_`) while removing trailing padding. For legacy email architectures, the MIME 76-character line wrap option splits continuous Base64 output with CRLF delimiters as specified in RFC 2045.\n\n## Browser-Only Security\nAll conversions occur locally in your browser memory. Uploaded binary files and sensitive encoded tokens are never transmitted to external APIs or logged to disk.",
  "base64-encoder/content/faqs.json": "{\n  \"schemaVersion\": 1,\n  \"items\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"how-does-utf8-base64-work\",\n      \"question\": \"Why is UTF-8 safe encoding important for Base64?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Standard browser btoa() throws an exception on Unicode characters like emojis and non-Latin scripts. Our tool uses TextEncoder and TextDecoder to ensure all Unicode characters are properly preserved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"what-is-url-safe-base64\",\n      \"question\": \"What is URL-safe Base64 encoding?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Standard Base64 uses \\\"+\\\" and \\\"/\\\" which have reserved meanings in URL parameters. URL-safe Base64 replaces \\\"+\\\" with \\\"-\\\" and \\\"/\\\" with \\\"_\\\", and omits trailing \\\"=\\\" padding characters.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"are-files-uploaded-to-server\",\n      \"question\": \"Are my files uploaded to a remote server when encoding?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Never. File reading and binary conversion occur entirely in your local browser memory using the FileReader and TypedArray APIs. Zero network requests are made.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"what-is-mime-line-wrapping\",\n      \"question\": \"What does the 76-character MIME wrap option do?\",\n      \"answer\": \"The MIME standard (RFC 2045) specifies that Base64 email attachments and headers should be split into lines of at most 76 characters separated by CRLF line breaks.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"how-to-decode-malformed-base64\",\n      \"question\": \"How does the decoder handle missing padding or URL-safe characters?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Our decoder automatically normalizes URL-safe characters and restores missing \\\"=\\\" padding before decoding, reporting clear syntax errors if invalid characters are detected.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"data-uri-scheme-usage\",\n      \"question\": \"What is a Data URI and how can I use it in HTML?\",\n      \"answer\": \"A Data URI embeds raw file data directly into HTML or CSS (data:image/png;base64,...) eliminating extra HTTP requests for small logos or icons.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"binary-chunking-memory\",\n      \"question\": \"How does the encoder handle large binary files without freezing?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Binary byte arrays are processed in discrete chunks of 3 bytes (yielding 4 ASCII characters), preventing memory stack overflows and browser thread locking.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"roundtrip-decoding-guarantee\",\n      \"question\": \"Is decoded text guaranteed to match the original input byte-for-byte?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Yes. Our Base64 engine undergoes automated roundtrip property tests proving that decode(encode(x)) strictly equals x for all arbitrary Unicode strings and byte buffers.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
