  "emi-calculator/content/explainer.md": "# How EMI Calculator Works\n\nThe Equated Monthly Installment (EMI) Calculator models loan amortizations under reducing-balance and flat-rate credit structures using arbitrary-precision decimal arithmetic.\n\n## Reducing Balance Formula\nFor standard mortgages, auto loans, and personal financing, the reducing balance monthly installment is computed as:\n$$\\text{EMI} = \\frac{P \\times r \\times (1 + r)^n}{(1 + r)^n - 1}$$\nwhere:\n- $P$ is the principal loan amount.\n- $r = \\frac{\\text{Annual Interest Rate}}{12 \\times 100}$ represents the periodic monthly interest rate.\n- $n = \\text{tenure in months}$.\n\n## Flat Rate Mode\nIn retail consumer electronics or vehicle dealer financing, lenders occasionally advertise \"flat rate\" interest. In flat-rate loans, interest is computed against the entire original principal regardless of ongoing repayments:\n$$\\text{EMI}_{\\text{flat}} = \\frac{P + \\left(P \\times \\frac{\\text{Rate}}{100} \\times \\text{Years}\\right)}{n}$$\nOur tool labels this mode explicitly to help borrowers identify the substantially higher effective interest cost of flat loans compared to diminishing balance financing.\n\n## South Asian (en-IN) Number Formatting\nWhen INR (₹) is selected, numeric figures format according to South Asian grouping conventions: the rightmost three digits are grouped together, followed by pairs of two digits (e.g. ₹ 1,00,000 for 1 Lakh and ₹ 1,00,00,000 for 1 Crore).\n\n## Amortization Schedule Integrity\nEach month, interest is calculated on the remaining balance: $\\text{interest} = \\text{round}(\\text{balance} \\times r, 2)$, and principal repayment is $\\text{EMI} - \\text{interest}$. The final payment adjusts by the few accumulated fractional cents to guarantee the ending balance is exactly $0.00$.\n\n## Important Financial Disclaimer\nThis EMI calculator is provided exclusively for personal educational and informational modeling purposes. It does not constitute formal financial, investment, lending, or tax advice. Actual loan terms, processing fees, insurance charges, and prepayment penalties vary across banking institutions. Always consult your financial advisor or lending officer before finalizing credit agreements.",
  "emi-calculator/content/faqs.json": "{\n  \"schemaVersion\": 1,\n  \"items\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"how-is-reducing-balance-emi-calculated\",\n      \"question\": \"How is reducing-balance EMI calculated?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Reducing-balance EMI uses the standard financial formula: EMI = [P × r × (1+r)^n] / [(1+r)^n - 1], where P is principal, r is the monthly interest rate (annual rate / 12 / 100), and n is the tenure in months.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"reducing-balance-vs-flat-rate\",\n      \"question\": \"What is the difference between reducing balance and flat rate?\",\n      \"answer\": \"In flat-rate loans, interest is charged on the original principal for the entire loan duration, resulting in a substantially higher effective interest cost compared to a reducing-balance loan of the same nominal rate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"indian-number-system-lakh-crore\",\n      \"question\": \"How does the Indian Lakh / Crore numbering system work?\",\n      \"answer\": \"When Indian Rupee (INR) is selected, figures are grouped in the South Asian format: 1,00,000 (1 Lakh) and 1,00,00,000 (1 Crore) using the en-IN formatting locale.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"can-i-download-the-schedule\",\n      \"question\": \"Can I download the month-by-month repayment schedule?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Yes. You can export the complete repayment schedule as a CSV spreadsheet detailing principal, interest, and outstanding balance for every installment.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"zero-interest-emi\",\n      \"question\": \"Does this calculator support 0% interest no-cost EMIs?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Yes. If the interest rate is set to 0%, the monthly EMI simplifies exactly to Principal divided by tenure without division-by-zero errors.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"final-installment-rounding-adjustment\",\n      \"question\": \"Why is the final monthly EMI sometimes a few cents different?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Because monthly payments are rounded to two decimal places, small fractional cent differences accumulate over decades. The final payment is adjusted to settle the remaining balance to exactly zero.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"principal-vs-interest-breakdown\",\n      \"question\": \"How does the principal-to-interest ratio change over time?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Early payments consist primarily of interest charges because the outstanding principal balance is at its highest. As principal is paid down, subsequent interest charges shrink steadily.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"arbitrary-precision-arithmetic\",\n      \"question\": \"How does this tool eliminate financial rounding errors?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Every calculation is processed using decimal.js with half-up rounding, eliminating IEEE 754 floating point arithmetic inaccuracies.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
