  "fake-name-generator/content/explainer.md": "# How Fake Name Generator Works\n\nThe Fake Name Generator synthesizes authentic-sounding fictional identities for software testing, automated QA pipelines, and design prototypes.\n\n## Cultural Name Modeling\nRather than calling third-party mock data services that can leak test requests, this utility bundles static linguistic dictionaries containing hundreds of verified given names and surnames across six major countries:\n- **United States & Canada:** Comprehensive blend of contemporary Anglo-Saxon, Latin, and international demographic names.\n- **United Kingdom & Australia:** Traditional and modern regional naming distributions.\n- **India & Pakistan:** Culturally authentic regional given names and surnames reflecting South Asian naming conventions.\n\n## Safe-By-Design Principles\n1. **RFC 2606 Reserved Domains:** All mock email addresses strictly utilize the reserved domain extensions `@example.com`, `@example.org`, and `@example.net`. These domains are hard-coded in internet routing protocols never to deliver mail, preventing accidental spam delivery.\n2. **Designated Fictional Numbers:** United States and Canadian telephone numbers strictly fall within the North American Numbering Plan (NANP) fictional allocation range `555-0100` through `555-0199`.\n3. **Strict Ban on PII:** This tool does not generate Social Security numbers, tax identifiers, passport credentials, or payment cards.\n\n## Deterministic Seeding & Export\nFor automated end-to-end testing, entering an integer seed initializes a Mulberry32 pseudorandom number generator. This guarantees reproducible sequences of test fixtures across test runs. Data can be exported to JSON or CSV format with a single click.\n\n## Practical Use Cases in Software Development\nDevelopment teams utilize mock identity records to populate relational databases, test complex form validations, verify international character encoding in name fields, and generate realistic sample data for stakeholder product demonstrations. Because names are drawn from authentic linguistic pools rather than random alphanumeric strings, prototype interfaces feel polished and user-friendly during user testing sessions.",
  "fake-name-generator/content/faqs.json": "{\n  \"schemaVersion\": 1,\n  \"items\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"is-generated-data-real\",\n      \"question\": \"Are these names and contact details connected to real individuals?\",\n      \"answer\": \"No. All generated identities are completely fictional combinations from bundled linguistic name datasets. Any resemblance to real living or deceased persons is purely coincidental.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"safe-phone-and-email-guarantees\",\n      \"question\": \"Why are emails and phone numbers guaranteed safe for development?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Emails are exclusively created using RFC 2606 reserved domains (example.com, example.org, example.net) which can never receive spam. US phone numbers use the fictional 555-0100 to 555-0199 test range.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"government-ids-policy\",\n      \"question\": \"Does this tool generate Social Security numbers, credit cards, or passports?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Strictly no. To prevent misuse, this utility never generates government identification numbers, financial credentials, or private citizen records.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"export-formats\",\n      \"question\": \"How can I export generated names for database seeding?\",\n      \"answer\": \"You can instantly copy data to your clipboard or download clean CSV and structured JSON files compatible with mock APIs and database seeding scripts.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"reproducible-seed-generation\",\n      \"question\": \"Can I generate the same set of test users across multiple runs?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Yes. By entering an optional numeric Seed value, the deterministic pseudorandom number generator produces the exact same sequence of names across test sessions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"demographic-localization\",\n      \"question\": \"How are culturally accurate names selected for each country?\",\n      \"answer\": \"The engine bundles curated dictionaries of over 200 first names and 200 family surnames for each supported country: US, UK, India, Pakistan, Canada, and Australia.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"gender-filtering-options\",\n      \"question\": \"Can I filter identities by gender?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Yes. You can select Male, Female, or Random to match the demographic requirements of your mock application or persona testing.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"date-of-birth-ranges\",\n      \"question\": \"What age brackets are used for simulated birth dates?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Dates of birth are generated within realistic adult ranges (ages 18 to 75) formatted as ISO 8601 YYYY-MM-DD strings.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
  "fake-name-generator/content/privacy.md": "## Privacy Policy for {{siteName}}\n\n**Effective Date:** {{effectiveDate}}\n\nAt {{siteName}} (available at {{domain}}), we take your privacy seriously. This Privacy Policy describes how we handle information when you visit or use {{toolName}}.\n\n## 1. Client-Side Only Processing\n\nAll computations, conversions, and data processing in {{toolName}} happen entirely within your web browser. \n\n- **No User Data Transmission:** Your inputs, values, files, or calculations are never sent to our servers or any third-party servers.\n- **No Remote Storage:** We do not collect, log, store, or transmit any data you enter into the tool.\n- **Data Retention:** Once you close or refresh your browser tab, all entered inputs and generated results are cleared from browser memory.\n\n## 2. Cookies and Local Storage\n\nThe site itself does not set or use tracking cookies.\n\n{{#if localStorage}}\n### Local Storage\nWe use browser local storage solely to remember non-identifying user interface preferences between visits. Specifically, we store the following keys:\n- `{{localStorageKeys}}`\n\nThis data remains stored locally on your device and is never transmitted to us. You can clear local storage at any time via your browser settings.\n{{/if}}\n\n{{#if ads}}\n### Advertising\nWe may display third-party advertisements to support the operation of this free utility. These advertising partners may use cookies, web beacons, or similar tracking technologies to serve ads based on your prior visits to this website or other sites on the Internet. You can manage your ad personalization settings or opt out of interest-based advertising by visiting [About Ads](https://www.aboutads.info/choices/) or [Network Advertising Initiative](https://www.networkadvertising.org/choices/).\n{{/if}}\n\n{{#if analytics}}\n### Analytics\nWe use privacy-respecting analytics to collect aggregated, anonymous traffic metrics (such as page views, referrer sources, and browser types) to understand how visitors use the site. No personal identifiers or calculation inputs are ever tracked or tied to your browsing session.\n{{/if}}\n\n## 3. Server Logging\n\nLike virtually all web hosts, the infrastructure hosting {{domain}} may record standard technical server logs. These logs may include your IP address, browser user-agent, requested URL, and access timestamps. These logs are maintained by our hosting provider for infrastructure security, DDoS mitigation, and operational integrity, and are not combined with any calculation data.\n\n## 4. Changes to This Policy\n\nWe may update this Privacy Policy from time to time. Any changes will be posted on this page with an updated effective date.\n\n## 5. Contact Us\n\nIf you have questions or concerns regarding this Privacy Policy, please contact us at:\n- **Email:** {{contactEmail}}\n- **Website:** {{domain}}\n\n",
