  "image-compressor/content/explainer.md": "# How Image Compressor Works\n\nThe Image Compressor reduces image file weight directly in your web browser using HTML5 Canvas rendering and browser-native lossy codecs.\n\n## Compression Mechanics\nWhen an image is dropped onto the tool, it is decoded into an in-memory bitmap using `createImageBitmap({ imageOrientation: 'from-image' })`. This API automatically applies orientation transforms specified in camera EXIF tags so photos never appear sideways.\n\nThe image is drawn to an off-screen `HTMLCanvasElement`. If optional dimension clamping is active (such as capping width at 2048 pixels), the canvas scales the image proportionally using high-quality bi-cubic interpolation. The canvas is then serialized using `canvas.toBlob(callback, format, quality)`:\n- **JPEG Encoding:** Uses discrete cosine transform (DCT) quantization across 8×8 pixel blocks.\n- **WebP Encoding:** Uses predictive intra-frame compression and variable-size macroblocks, often outperforming JPEG by 25–35% at identical perceived visual quality.\n\n## Strict Size Protection\nA frequent pitfall of automated compression is re-encoding an already heavily compressed image at a higher quality, resulting in an output file larger than the original. Our engine compares the post-compression byte count against the source file. If the output is not strictly smaller, the original file is preserved and flagged clearly.\n\n## Percentage Savings and Batch Export\nFile size savings are measured as a direct percentage reduction: $\\text{Savings} = \\left(\\frac{\\text{Original Bytes} - \\text{Compressed Bytes}}{\\text{Original Bytes}}\\right) \\times 100$. For instance, reducing a 2,000,000-byte digital camera capture down to 500,000 bytes achieves an exact 75.0% file weight reduction. Users compressing dozens of images can download compressed assets individually or package the entire queue into an uncompressed ZIP container using fast client-side Deflate compression.\n\n## Metadata Stripping & Privacy\nCanvas serialization generates a pure pixel raster stream that contains zero EXIF metadata. Embedded camera serial numbers, lens profiles, exposure details, and GPS coordinates are stripped completely before download. No image bytes are sent over the network.",
  "image-compressor/content/faqs.json": "{\n  \"schemaVersion\": 1,\n  \"items\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"how-does-client-side-compression-work\",\n      \"question\": \"How does client-side image compression work without server uploads?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Images are drawn to an HTML5 Canvas using createImageBitmap and re-encoded directly in browser memory at your designated quality setting. Zero bytes are uploaded to any server.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"why-are-exif-metadata-stripped\",\n      \"question\": \"Are EXIF metadata and GPS locations stripped during compression?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Yes. Drawing an image to a canvas naturally strips all EXIF metadata, camera settings, and geolocation tags, enhancing privacy while reducing file weight.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"what-happens-if-compressed-file-is-larger\",\n      \"question\": \"What happens if the compressed result is larger than the original?\",\n      \"answer\": \"If re-encoding does not yield a smaller file size (common when attempting to re-compress already heavily optimized files), the tool automatically preserves your original file and alerts you.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"which-formats-supported\",\n      \"question\": \"Which image formats are supported for compression?\",\n      \"answer\": \"JPEG, WebP, and PNG images are supported. You can re-encode JPEGs and WebPs with custom quality factors or convert PNGs to modern WebP.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"batch-compression-support\",\n      \"question\": \"Can I compress multiple images at once?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Yes. You can drag and drop multiple files to compress them simultaneously and download them individually or as a ZIP archive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"quality-slider-guidelines\",\n      \"question\": \"What quality setting offers the best balance of file size and visual fidelity?\",\n      \"answer\": \"A quality factor between 0.70 and 0.80 typically reduces JPEG and WebP file weight by 60% to 80% with virtually imperceptible visual compression artifacts on web screens.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"dimension-clamping-benefits\",\n      \"question\": \"Why should I set a maximum pixel dimension?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Modern smartphone cameras capture images at 4000+ pixels wide. Constraining resolution to 1920 or 2048 pixels yields dramatic file size reductions before compression even begins.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"memory-management-large-images\",\n      \"question\": \"Can this tool process high-resolution DSLR photos safely?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Yes. The engine uses createImageBitmap and automatically revokes object URLs upon completion, ensuring browser memory is freed immediately.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
